---
/**
 * About: who the church is, in the church's own words.
 *
 * Every word of substance on this page is lifted from what Brookside already
 * publishes about itself (brooksidechurchofgod.com/what-we-believe and /staff),
 * not written for it. That is deliberate. An earlier draft of this page was
 * generated by a design tool and asserted a founding year of 1948, three
 * pastors across the church's history, a borrowed schoolhouse, four building
 * expansions and a white-frame building still standing. None of it appears on
 * the church's own site, and none of it was ever confirmed with anyone at the
 * church. It is not here. If real history surfaces later it can be added; an
 * invented history could not be taken back once people read it.
 *
 * The same restraint applies to the doctrine. The old draft paraphrased the
 * church's beliefs into six tidy lines of its own composition. A church's
 * doctrinal statement is not ours to rewrite, so the four headings below
 * (Christian, Protestant, Foundational, Evangelical) and the five foundational
 * doctrines are the Church of God's published wording, lightly trimmed for
 * length and never for meaning.
 *
 * The structural device is those four words. Watch uses the year as its rail;
 * here it is the church's own vocabulary for itself, set in display type so the
 * page is carried by what the church actually claims rather than by stock
 * imagery, which is just as well because there is none.
 *
 * No crossAt: the crossbar is Home's payoff and repeating it everywhere spends
 * it. The line runs as a plain spine here, as it does on Watch.
 */
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import data from '../data/sermons.json';

const beliefs = [
	{
		term: 'Christian',
		body:
			'First and foremost, the Church of God is a determinedly Christian church. It is built upon the person of Jesus Christ, the Son of God, and the doctrines and practices of the church are based upon His teachings.',
	},
	{
		term: 'Protestant',
		body:
			'Founded upon the principles of Protestantism, though not a traditional follower of any one leader of the Reformation. We stand firmly for justification by faith, the priesthood of believers, the authority of the Bible, religious freedom, and the separation of church and state.',
	},
	{
		term: 'Foundational',
		body: 'The Church of God subscribes to five foundational Christian doctrines.',
		list: [
			'The inerrancy and infallibility of the Bible.',
			'The virgin birth and complete deity of Christ.',
			"The atoning sacrifice of Christ's death for the sins of the world.",
			'The literal resurrection of the body.',
			"Christ's second coming in bodily form to earth.",
		],
	},
	{
		term: 'Evangelical',
		body:
			'Evangelical describes those who affirm the primary doctrines revealed in the Scriptures: the inspiration and authority of the Word of God, the Trinity, the deity and virgin birth of Jesus Christ, salvation by faith in the atoning death of Christ, His bodily resurrection and ascension, the ministry of the Holy Spirit, the second coming of Christ, and the spiritual unity of believers.',
	},
];

/*
 * Bios.
 *
 * The live site's versions are inconsistent in person (Brad's third, the other
 * two first) and two of them close on details that have aged out: a son
 * "starting college at Samford" and a "baby girl". Rewritten at Caleb's
 * direction into one register, third person, ministry first.
 *
 * Marcie is described as the pastor's wife who serves across the church, at
 * Caleb's direction on 2026-07-28. She carries a "Ladies Ministry Director"
 * title with a church email on the live site's contact page but appears on no
 * staff page; the title was not the framing Caleb chose, so it is not used.
 *
 * What survived from the live site is only what is durable and published:
 * Brad and Marcie joining in 1999, their two sons, Daniel's 1996 licensing and
 * 1999 ordination and his wife Patti. Note "joined" is deliberate and is the
 * church's own word. It is a weaker claim than "senior pastor since 1999" and
 * nobody has confirmed the stronger one, so it stays weak.
 *
 * `photo` is null until a real portrait exists. See the note on .who__frame.
 *
 * Daniel's was the first one. It was salvaged off the Squarespace CDN on
 * 2026-07-29 (the old /staff page, filename `DanielHowardBio.jpg`) and dies with
 * the account, so the copy in `branding/brookside-church/photography/source/
 * salvage-squarespace/` in Compass is the only one. Source is 750x743; the 4:5
 * master beside it is the largest honest crop at 594x743. That is 1.24x the
 * 480x600 slot, not 2x, so it is slightly soft on a retina screen and there is
 * no better source. Do NOT upscale it to fake the missing pixels.
 *
 * It is greyscale, and .who__photo desaturates every portrait to match, so a
 * colour phone photo can be dropped in later without the row splitting into two
 * treatments. See the note on .who__photo.
 *
 * Brad's and Caleb's arrived 2026-08-10 and are NOT solo portraits: Caleb chose
 * to keep the whole family in frame, so these are a couple and a family of four.
 * That decision is what makes them usable at all. Cropped to one person they
 * came out at 0.48x and 0.51x of the slot and would have needed a 2x upscale;
 * used whole, the largest 4:5 crop is 520x650 and 724x905, which is 1.08x and
 * 1.51x, so neither is upscaled. Sources are in `photography/source/` in Compass.
 *
 * The consequence to know before "fixing" it: face scale is now inconsistent
 * across the row, because Daniel's is a tight head-and-shoulders and the others
 * are full-length. Daniel's cannot be loosened (750x743 is the whole source) and
 * the others cannot be tightened without either cutting someone out or dropping
 * back under the slot. It is a real trade and this is the side of it that keeps
 * every face at native resolution.
 *
 * `alt` overrides the generated "Name, Role" string. It exists because that
 * string is a lie on a group photo: the frame holds four people, not a pastor.
 */
const staff = [
	{
		name: 'Brad Lindsey',
		role: 'Senior Pastor',
		photo: '/staff-brad-lindsey.webp',
		alt: 'Brad Lindsey, Senior Pastor, with his wife Marcie',
		bio:
			'Brad and his wife, Marcie, joined Brookside Church of God in 1999, and have seen God’s hand move sovereignly through their lives and the church in the years since. He has traveled nationally and internationally in missions work. Marcie serves across many areas of the church. Brad and Marcie have two sons, Caleb and Cole.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Daniel Howard',
		role: 'Teaching Pastor',
		photo: '/staff-daniel-howard.webp',
		alt: 'Daniel Howard, Teaching Pastor',
		bio:
			'Daniel was licensed in 1996 and ordained into the ministry in 1999. A self-proclaimed Bible nerd and treasure hunter, his greatest joy is digging into the Word, turning up the gold in it, and handing it to the church. He and his wife, Patti, have one son.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Caleb Lindsey',
		role: 'Worship Pastor',
		photo: '/staff-caleb-lindsey.webp',
		alt: 'Caleb Lindsey, Worship Pastor, with his wife and their two daughters',
		bio:
			'Caleb leads the church into the presence of God through authentic worship, and counts it a privilege to use what God has given him to advance the Gospel from week to week. He and his wife have two daughters.',
	},
	{
		/*
		 * Added 2026-08-10. Surname is inferred, not published: Brad's bio names
		 * "two sons, Caleb and Cole" and Caleb is a Lindsey. Confirm before the
		 * DNS cutover if it has not been already.
		 *
		 * NEEDS CONFIRM: this bio is the shortest one on the page because it is
		 * built only from what is actually known — the role Caleb gave, and
		 * Aubrey's name. Everything that would round it out to the length of the
		 * other three (when he started, how long he has been at Brookside, what
		 * he does outside the role) would have to be invented, so it is not
		 * written. Replace it with Cole's own words rather than padding it.
		 */
		name: 'Cole Lindsey',
		role: 'Youth Pastor',
		photo: '/staff-cole-lindsey.webp',
		alt: 'Cole Lindsey, Youth Pastor, with his wife Aubrey',
		bio:
			'Cole and his wife, Aubrey, serve the students of Brookside Church of God.',
	},
	{
		/*
		 * Added 2026-08-10. The only joint entry on the page: Corey and Megan hold
		 * the role together, so this is one card naming two people rather than a
		 * pastor with a spouse alongside. `role` is plural for the same reason and
		 * is not a typo — do not "correct" it to Children's Pastor.
		 *
		 * NEEDS CONFIRM, same as Cole's: the bio is one sentence because the role
		 * and the two names are all that is actually known. Replace it with their
		 * own words rather than padding it out to match the longer three.
		 */
		name: 'Corey and Megan Jackson',
		role: 'Children’s Pastors',
		photo: '/staff-corey-megan-jackson.webp',
		alt: 'Corey and Megan Jackson, Children’s Pastors',
		bio:
			'Corey and Megan Jackson serve the children of Brookside Church of God.',
	},
];

/*
 * The senior pastor is featured on his own row above the others. Taken by
 * position rather than by name or role so that featuring somebody else is a
 * reorder of `staff` and nothing else; the array is already in seniority order.
 */
const [lead, ...rest] = staff;

/** First letters of the first and last name, for the portrait placeholder. */
const initials = (name: string) =>
	name.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).map((w) => w[0]).join('').slice(0, 2).toUpperCase();

const months = ['January','February','March','April','May','June','July','August','September','October','November','December'];
const monthYear = (iso: string) => {
	const [y, m] = iso.split('-');
	return `${months[Number(m) - 1]} ${y}`;
};
---

<SiteLayout
	title="About"
	description="Brookside Church of God is a small church on Cardiff Street in Brookside, Alabama. What we believe, who leads, and what happens on a Sunday."
>
	<section class="bay atop">
		<p class="eyebrow">About &middot; Brookside Church of God</p>
		<h1 class="display" data-split>
			A church<br />built on one<br /><em>person</em>
		</h1>
		<div class="atop__foot">
			{/*
				"Pentecostal" is load-bearing, not decoration. Brookside is Church of
				God (Cleveland, Tennessee), which is a Pentecostal denomination, and
				the church names itself that way first on its own Facebook profile.
				A visitor deciding whether to walk in on Sunday needs it, and leaving
				it out to sound broader would be the actual error.
			*/}
			<p class="lede">
				A small Pentecostal congregation on Cardiff Street in Brookside,
				Alabama, and a church family we would love you to be part of. Come as
				you are and sit wherever you like.
			</p>
			<dl class="hours">
				<div><dt>Sunday School</dt><dd>9:45 AM</dd></div>
				<div><dt>Morning Worship</dt><dd>10:45 AM</dd></div>
				<div><dt>Wednesday Classes</dt><dd>6:30 PM</dd></div>
			</dl>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="bay rule creed" aria-labelledby="creed-heading">
		<p class="eyebrow" id="creed-heading">What we believe</p>
		<h2 class="headline creed__head">
			Four words the Church of God uses to describe itself.
		</h2>
		{/* The church's own line, from its Facebook profile. Worth keeping: it is
		    the sentence that tells a visitor from another tradition they can come. */}
		<p class="lede creed__welcome">We welcome people from all denominations.</p>
		<dl class="creed__list">
			{beliefs.map((b) => (
				<div class="creed__row">
					<dt class="creed__term">{b.term}</dt>
					<dd class="creed__body">
						<p class="prose">{b.body}</p>
						{b.list && (
							<ol class="creed__five">
								{b.list.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
							</ol>
						)}
					</dd>
				</div>
			))}
		</dl>
	</section>

	<section class="bay rule mission" aria-labelledby="mission-heading">
		<p class="eyebrow" id="mission-heading">Why we are here</p>
		<p class="mission__text">
			To preach and support the Gospel of Jesus Christ both locally and abroad,
			opening the door for lives to be changed and transformed through the power
			of Jesus Christ.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="bay rule team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
		<p class="eyebrow" id="team-heading">Who leads</p>
		{/*
			Deliberately not "three pastors": that phrase, meaning three across the
			church's whole history, was one of the invented claims on the old draft,
			and echoing it here would quietly reintroduce it. This heading counts
			nobody.
		*/}
		<h2 class="headline team__head">The people who preach and lead here.</h2>
		{/*
			The church does not publish a founding year and nobody has been able to
			confirm one, so this stands in for it at Caleb's direction: 1999 is
			verifiable from the church's own staff page and, unlike "27 years", it
			never goes stale. Do not convert it to a number of years.
		*/}
		<p class="lede team__since">
			Brad and Marcie Lindsey have been at Brookside since 1999.
		</p>
		{/*
			The senior pastor takes his own row above the rest, at Caleb's direction
			2026-08-10. Split off by position, not by name or title, so reordering the
			array is the only thing needed to feature someone else — and `staff[0]` is
			Brad because the array is already in seniority order.

			Both cards share the same `.who` classes and the same `.who__text` wrapper,
			deliberately: `.who--lead` is a layout modifier only. That keeps one markup
			shape, so the audit's existing /about selectors still cover the lead card
			instead of silently skipping it, which is exactly how /watch once "passed"
			an audit that asserted nothing.
		*/}
		<div class="team__lead">
			<article class="who who--lead">
				<div class="who__frame">
					{lead.photo
						? <img class="who__photo" src={lead.photo} alt={lead.alt} width="480" height="600" loading="lazy" />
						: <span class="who__initials" aria-hidden="true">{initials(lead.name)}</span>}
				</div>
				<div class="who__text">
					<p class="who__role">{lead.role}</p>
					<h3 class="who__name">{lead.name}</h3>
					<p class="prose who__bio">{lead.bio}</p>
				</div>
			</article>
		</div>
		<div class="team__grid">
			{rest.map((p) => (
				<article class="who">
					<div class="who__frame">
						{p.photo
							? <img class="who__photo" src={p.photo} alt={p.alt} width="480" height="600" loading="lazy" />
							: <span class="who__initials" aria-hidden="true">{initials(p.name)}</span>}
					</div>
					<div class="who__text">
						<p class="who__role">{p.role}</p>
						<h3 class="who__name">{p.name}</h3>
						<p class="prose who__bio">{p.bio}</p>
					</div>
				</article>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="bay rule listen" aria-labelledby="listen-heading">
		<p class="eyebrow" id="listen-heading">Hear it for yourself</p>
		<h2 class="headline listen__head">
			{data.total} sermons, back to {monthYear(data.oldest)}.
		</h2>
		<p class="lede listen__lede">
			The clearest picture of a church is what it preaches week after week. All
			of it is free, and none of it is edited down.
		</p>
		<div class="actions listen__actions">
			<a class="btn btn--solid" href="/visit/">Plan your visit</a>
			<a class="btn btn--line" href="/watch/">Browse the archive</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</SiteLayout>

<style is:global>
	/* Page-level composition only. Anything reusable belongs in site.css. */
	.atop { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5.5rem) clamp(3rem, 7vw, 4.5rem); }
	.atop__foot {
		display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
		align-items: start; margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
	}
	.atop__foot .lede { flex: 1 1 24rem; }
	.atop__foot .hours { margin-left: auto; flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 15rem; }

	/* ---------- beliefs ---------- */

	/*
		Asymmetric bottom padding on purpose: the last creed row ends on a rule and
		the mission band opens with one, so a symmetric block here stacks two
		horizontals a hair apart and reads as a mistake.
	*/
	.creed { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem) clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem); }
	.creed__head { max-width: 20ch; margin-bottom: 1.1rem; }
	/* Sits between the heading and the first term, so it needs to be clearly
	   attached to the heading and clearly clear of the rail. */
	.creed__welcome { margin: 0 0 clamp(2.25rem, 4.5vw, 3.25rem); }
	.creed__list { margin: 0; }
	.creed__row {
		display: grid; gap: 0.5rem clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1.6fr);
		align-items: start;
		padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
		border-top: 1px solid var(--ash);
	}
	/* No border-bottom on the last row: the mission band's .rule is that line. */
	/*
		The term carries the page, so it is set on the display face rather than as
		a heading weight. It is a <dt>, so the relationship to its body text stays
		in the markup and is not just a visual pairing.
	*/
	.creed__term {
		font-family: var(--display);
		font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.6vw, 2.9rem);
		font-variation-settings: 'wght' 700, 'opsz' 32;
		line-height: 1; letter-spacing: -0.035em;
	}
	.creed__body { margin: 0; }
	/*
		Steps up from the shared .prose size. This column is the doctrine itself,
		and at 0.96rem against a 2.9rem term it read as a caption on the term
		rather than as the substance of the section.
	*/
	.creed__body .prose {
		margin: 0; max-width: 54ch;
		font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.15vw, 1.08rem); line-height: 1.62; color: var(--ink);
	}
	.creed__five {
		margin: 1rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none;
		counter-reset: doctrine;
	}
	.creed__five li {
		counter-increment: doctrine;
		display: flex; gap: 0.85rem; align-items: baseline;
		padding: 0.5rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--ash);
		color: var(--iron); font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.55;
	}
	.creed__five li::before {
		content: counter(doctrine, decimal-leading-zero);
		flex: none; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.68rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--coral);
	}

	/* ---------- mission ---------- */

	.mission { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem); }
	.mission__text {
		margin: 0; max-width: 34ch; font-family: var(--display);
		font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.6vw, 2rem);
		font-variation-settings: 'wght' 500, 'opsz' 24;
		line-height: 1.25; letter-spacing: -0.025em; text-wrap: pretty;
	}

	/* ---------- staff ---------- */

	.team { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
	.team__head { max-width: 22ch; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
	.team__since { margin: 0 0 clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.75rem); }
	.team__grid {
		display: grid; gap: clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 3rem);
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr));
	}
	.team__lead { margin-bottom: clamp(2.25rem, 4.5vw, 3.5rem); }
	/*
		The lead card is wider than the others but its PHOTO is deliberately not much
		wider, and the ceiling is arithmetic rather than taste. Brad's source is
		520x650, so 2x retina caps the frame at 260px CSS; the grid cards already
		render at 241px. Anything past ~260px is served below native and goes soft on
		a retina screen, and it is the one portrait with no better original.

		So the card grows the way the source allows: the frame goes to the ceiling,
		and the extra prominence comes from the row to itself, the larger name, and a
		bio set at reading width instead of squeezed into a 241px column. If a
		higher-resolution original ever arrives, raise the 260px and nothing else
		here has to change.
	*/
	.who--lead {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 260px) minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
		align-items: start;
	}
	.who--lead .who__frame { margin-bottom: 0; }
	.who--lead .who__role { font-size: 0.74rem; }
	.who--lead .who__name {
		font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 2.4vw, 2.15rem); margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
	}
	.who--lead .who__bio { max-width: 62ch; }
	.who { border-top: 2px solid var(--ink); padding-top: 0.9rem; }

	/*
		Portrait placeholder, standing in until real staff photographs are shot as
		part of this upgrade. Set `photo` on the entry and the <img> replaces the
		initials with no other change: the frame already owns the aspect ratio, so
		swapping in a real portrait cannot shift the layout.

		Deliberately not a grey box. An empty grey rectangle reads as an image that
		failed to load, which makes a finished page look broken; initials set in
		the display face read as a decision. Same reason it is ash on paper rather
		than a mid grey, and why nothing here says "photo coming soon".
	*/
	.who__frame {
		aspect-ratio: 4 / 5; margin-bottom: 1rem;
		display: grid; place-items: center; overflow: hidden;
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ash) 26%, var(--paper));
	}
	/*
		Every portrait renders greyscale, deliberately, and it is not a style
		flourish. The only portrait that exists is Daniel's, salvaged greyscale
		off the old site; the rest will arrive as colour phone photos taken by
		different people at different times. Desaturating at the slot means the
		row reads as one set however mixed the sources are, instead of splitting
		into "the old scanned one" and "the new phone ones".

		It also keeps coral doing one job. Coral is the site's only accent and it
		marks a line everywhere else; skin tones and a warm colour background
		compete with it directly at this size.

		One line to reverse if real portraits are ever shot together.
	*/
	.who__photo {
		width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; display: block;
		filter: grayscale(1);
	}
	.who__initials {
		font-family: var(--display);
		font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 3.4rem);
		font-variation-settings: 'wght' 700, 'opsz' 40;
		letter-spacing: -0.04em; line-height: 1;
		color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ash) 88%, var(--iron));
	}
	.who__role {
		margin: 0 0 0.55rem; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.68rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--iron);
	}
	.who__name {
		margin: 0 0 0.7rem; font-family: var(--display);
		font-size: 1.35rem; font-variation-settings: 'wght' 700;
		letter-spacing: -0.025em;
	}
	.who__bio { margin: 0; }

	/* ---------- closing ---------- */

	.listen { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
	.listen__head { max-width: 18ch; }
	.listen__lede { margin-top: 1.1rem; }
	.listen__actions { margin-top: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.25rem); }

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.atop__foot .hours { width: 100%; margin-left: 0; }
		.creed__row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.75rem; }
		/*
			Stacked, and the frame is capped rather than set to 100%. A phone column is
			wider than 260px, so letting the lead photo fill it would push the one
			portrait with the weakest source furthest past native — the opposite of
			what the desktop cap is for.
		*/
		.who--lead { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.1rem; }
		.who--lead .who__frame { max-width: 260px; }
	}
</style>

<script>
	import gsap from 'gsap';
	import ScrollTrigger from 'gsap/ScrollTrigger';
	import SplitText from 'gsap/SplitText';
	import Lenis from 'lenis';

	gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger, SplitText);

	if (!window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
		const lenis = new Lenis({ duration: 1.05 });
		lenis.on('scroll', ScrollTrigger.update);
		gsap.ticker.add((t) => lenis.raf(t * 1000));
		gsap.ticker.lagSmoothing(0);

		const heading = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-split]');
		if (heading) {
			const split = new SplitText(heading, { type: 'lines', linesClass: 'line', mask: 'lines' });
			gsap.from(split.lines, {
				yPercent: 130, duration: 1.1, ease: 'power4.out', stagger: 0.09, delay: 0.1,
			});
		}

		gsap.from('.atop .eyebrow, .atop__foot', {
			opacity: 0, y: 16, duration: 0.75, ease: 'power2.out', stagger: 0.1, delay: 0.4,
		});

		// Reveals start high in the viewport so anything below the fold is already
		// on screen by the time it plays: nothing sits at zero opacity waiting for
		// a trigger that might never fire.
		gsap.utils.toArray<HTMLElement>(
			'.creed__head, .creed__row, .mission__text, .team__head, .team__since, .who, .listen > *',
		).forEach((el) => {
			gsap.from(el, {
				opacity: 0, y: 24, duration: 0.7, ease: 'power2.out',
				scrollTrigger: { trigger: el, start: 'top 92%', once: true },
			});
		});
	}
</script>
